## Test Smell Audit — Academic 19-Smell Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`  
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 (.NET 10) · **Category:** Integration (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell 3)

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← 3-second hard sleep
```

**Why it's bad even in integration tests:** Introduces 3 seconds of wall-clock time per run, creates false failures under load, and doesn't actually wait for the *condition*—just hopes it's done.

**Fix:**
```csharp
// Poll or use a signaling mechanism:
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Integration calibration:** Acknowledged — some async coordination is expected, but `Thread.Sleep` with a fixed 3 s delay is still flagged because a polling/event-based approach is strictly better. Severity remains High per Spadini et al. (MSR 2020) developer perception data.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell 1)

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why it's bad:** One branch always goes un-exercised. If the production code changes the return type, the test silently asserts something different — it can never *fail* on the untaken path.

**Fix — split into two focused tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    // arrange a standard user
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    // arrange a premium user explicitly
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("premium@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell 4)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← No assertion — passes even if inserts silently discard data
}
```

**Note:** Name implies "does not throw" intent, but the framework already fails on unhandled exceptions — this adds no value beyond what the test runner provides for free.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var all = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count);
}
```

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

4 assertions, zero descriptive messages. A failure reports only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` — no domain context.

**Fix — add messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Integration calibration:** Severity stays Medium (not downgraded) — assertion clarity is framework-agnostic.

---

### ✅ Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Written Tests)

| Test | What's Good |
|------|-------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange/act/assert, focused, good assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Negative assertion (`Assert.IsNull`) proves deletion |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count is contextually obvious (3 adds → 3 count); `CollectionAssert` used well |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good concurrency stress test with proper assertion |

The fixture itself is well-scoped: in-memory SQLite is hermetic (no **Mystery Guest**), and both fields are used by every test (no **General Fixture**).

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | ~1 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into 2 deterministic tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message parameters to 4 asserts | ~2 min |

Fix #1 and #3 first — they directly cause **false confidence** and **flakiness**, the two highest-cost defects in CI pipelines (Camara et al., SAST 2021).
